{"obligations":{"reduction":{"status":"written_and_valid","quote":null,"witness":null,"load_bearing":null},"lower_bound":{"status":"written_but_invalid","quote":"If all prime factors of \(x\) are at most \(A\), then \(\sigma(x)=P(x)\), which is good by (1).","witness":"Counterexample: take A=15 and x=18. The greedy sequence begins 15,18,20: 16 and 17 are coprime to 15; 18 shares 3 with 15; 19 is inadmissible; and 20 shares 5 with 15 and 2 with 18. All prime factors of 18 are at most 15, but Q={3,5}, so σ(18)={3} whereas P(18)={2,3}. Moreover σ(18) is not good because it misses P(20)={2,5}.","load_bearing":"This false step supplies a branch of the induction proving the large-prime-erasure lemma. Without that lemma, the claimed reduction of membership to finitely many primes is unsupported."},"upper_bound":{"status":"written_but_invalid","quote":"Hence \(\sigma(m+L)=\sigma(m)\), and (3) gives
\[
m\text{ is a sequence term}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
m+L\text{ is a sequence term}. \tag{4}
\]","witness":"Counterexample: for A=15 the write-up defines L=3·5=15. As verified above, 18 is a term, but 33 is not: 20 is an earlier term and gcd(33,20)=1. Thus 18 is a term while 18+L=33 is not, directly contradicting (4).","load_bearing":"Equation (4) is the sole basis for counting a constant number T of terms in every length-L window and concluding a_{n+T}=a_n+L from n=1."}},"S_valid":0,"wrong_answer_stated":true,"notes":"The proof mistakenly uses primes dividing A, although its own A=15 example shows that primes such as 2 also matter. The later claim that all primes at most A are used is not implemented in the definitions or lemma."}
